.TH WASH 1 "2026" "wash" "wash manual"
.SH NAME
wash \- lightweight remote-admin desktop environment (multi-call binary)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wash
.RI [ subcommand ]
.br
.B wash-<app>
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wash
is a transport-only router and a family of web-component apps that present a
Windows/GNOME/macOS-style desktop over HTTP/WS, for maintaining Linux machines
over the network.
.PP
It is shipped as a single
.B busybox\-style multi-call binary:
.I /usr/bin/wash
dispatches on its invocation name (argv[0]), and each
.I /usr/bin/wash-<app>
is a symlink to it. Running a symlink launches that applet; running
.B wash
directly accepts subcommands.
.PP
The multi-user front door
.RB ( wash-login )
and the privileged sudo helper
.RB ( wash-sudo )
are separate real binaries, not symlinks.
.SH OPTIONS
Each applet accepts its own options. Two are common to all:
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print applet usage and exit.
.SH APPLETS
The set of applets matches the
.I wash-*
symlinks under
.IR /usr/bin .
Notable entry points:
.TP
.B wash-router
The transport router (single-user entry point).
.TP
.B wash-login
Multi-user authenticated login front door (separate package
.BR wash-login ).
.TP
.B wash-term ", " wash-fm ", " wash-edit ", " wash-settings ", ...
Desktop applications. Run
.B wash-<app> --help
for per-applet usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/wash
The multi-call dispatcher.
.TP
.I /etc/default/wash-login
Arguments for the wash-login service (when the wash-login package is installed).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wash-login (1),
.B /usr/share/doc/wash/MULTIUSER.md
